@startuml stock-reservation-race

' Replaces the t1..t10 ASCII interleaving table in
' explanation/concurrency-and-locking.md, "The failure". Same scenario,
' same timeline — two customers checking out on the last item,
' current = 1, reserved = 0, no lock held.

title Stock reservation — the race the lock exists to close

participant "Request A" as A
participant "Request B" as B
database "inventories\n(current=1, reserved=0)" as DB

A -> DB : BEGIN
A -> DB : SELECT → reserved = 0
activate A

B -> DB : BEGIN
B -> DB : SELECT → reserved = 0
activate B

note over A, B
  **Both read before either wrote.**
  Both decided on state that was true
  when read and false once acted on.
  This IS the check-then-act pattern —
  the window between the two SELECTs
  and the two UPDATEs below is where
  every version of this bug lives.
end note

A -> A : available = 1 - 0 = 1 >= 1\n**proceed**
B -> B : available = 1 - 0 = 1 >= 1\n**proceed**

A -> DB : UPDATE reserved = 1
A -> DB : COMMIT
deactivate A

B -> DB : UPDATE reserved = 1
B -> DB : COMMIT
deactivate B

note over DB
  **Oversold.** Both requests reserved the
  same single unit. reserved=1 satisfies
  chk_inventories_reserved_not_above_current
  (it never went above current), so the
  CHECK constraint — which catches a stale
  read-modify-write — cannot catch THIS
  failure, because current never got
  compared against two DIFFERENT reservation
  attempts, only the final value.
end note

@enduml
